<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Story of Christmas</h1><p class="path-tagline">From the Nativity accounts and the choice of 25 December to the customs of the feast.</p><p class="path-intro">Christmas commemorates the birth of Jesus, told in the Gospels of Matthew and Luke, which state no date. The earliest document to place the birth on 25 December was written in Rome in 336; the day was the traditional date of the winter solstice in the Roman Empire. These stops begin with the faith and the Nativity accounts, then follow the customs that gathered around the feast: the manger scene attributed to Francis of Assisi in 1223, carols, the tree first recorded among German Lutherans in the 16th century, and the gift-bringer who grew from a 4th-century bishop of Myra into Santa Claus. The last stop is Dickens's novella of 1843.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/christianity"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 30 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Christianity</span><span class="path-step-why">The faith began among the followers of Jesus of Nazareth, a Jewish teacher crucified around 30 CE, and became the religion of the Roman Empire in the 4th century.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Christianity is a monotheistic religion based on the life and teachings of Jesus of Nazareth, which arose in the first century CE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-of-jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nativity-of-jesus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Event · c. 6–4 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Nativity of Jesus</span><span class="path-step-why">Matthew and Luke agree that Jesus was born in Bethlehem in the reign of Herod but state no date; only Matthew has the wise men, only Luke the shepherds.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Nativity of Jesus is the birth of Jesus as told in the Gospels of Matthew and Luke, the basis of the Christian holiday of Christmas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/magi"><span class="path-step-ph">3</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · People</span><span class="path-step-name">Magi</span><span class="path-step-why">The magi were priests of Zoroastrianism and earlier Iranian religions; in Matthew, magi from the east pay homage to the infant Jesus, rendered in English as kings or wise men.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The magi (singular magus) were priests in Zoroastrianism and earlier Iranian religions.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/christmas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Christmas</span><span class="path-step-why">The earliest document to place the birth on 25 December was written in Rome in 336; the day was the traditional date of the winter solstice in the Roman Empire.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Christmas is a religious and cultural celebration commemorating the birth of Jesus Christ, observed primarily on 25 December; part of the Eastern Christian churches keep it on 25 December of the Julian calendar, which currently corresponds to 7 January.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 4th century</span><span class="path-step-name">Saint Nicholas</span><span class="path-step-why">The 4th-century bishop of Myra was venerated as a wonder-worker and patron of sailors and children, and from 1087 his shrine at Bari drew pilgrims.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Saint Nicholas was a 4th-century bishop of Myra in Lycia, in present-day Turkey, venerated across Eastern and Western Christianity as a wonder-worker and patron of sailors and children.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-scene"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nativity-scene.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Nativity scene</span><span class="path-step-why">The first seasonal manger scene, enacted rather than sculpted, is attributed to Francis of Assisi, who is said to have staged it in a cave near Greccio in 1223.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A nativity scene, also called a manger scene, crib or crèche, is a display, particularly during Christmastide, of figures representing the birth of Jesus.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-carol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Christmas carol</span><span class="path-step-why">Christmas hymns may be traced to 4th-century Rome and the earliest English carols to a manuscript of 1426; carols grew in popularity after the Reformation.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A Christmas carol is a song or hymn on the theme of Christmas, traditionally sung at Christmas or in the surrounding season.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-tree"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-tree.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Christmas tree</span><span class="path-step-why">The decorated evergreen was first recorded among German Lutherans in the 16th century, and in Britain Queen Charlotte introduced a tree in 1800.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A Christmas tree is a decorated tree, usually an evergreen conifer such as a spruce, pine or fir, set up for the celebration of Christmas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-claus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Santa Claus</span><span class="path-step-why">The gift-bringer of Christmas Eve derives from Saint Nicholas and the Dutch Sinterklaas; an 1823 poem with a sleigh and flying reindeer laid the foundation of his modern image.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Santa Claus is a legendary figure originating in Western Christian culture who is said to bring gifts on the night of Christmas Eve.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/a-christmas-carol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/a-christmas-carol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1843</span><span class="path-step-name">A Christmas Carol</span><span class="path-step-why">Dickens's novella of the miser Scrooge and the three spirits was published on 19 December 1843, sold out by Christmas Eve and has never been out of print.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A Christmas Carol is a novella by Charles Dickens, first published in London by Chapman &amp; Hall in 1843 with illustrations by John Leech.</span></span></a></li></ol>
